WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.471 --> 00:00:04.980
CEOs know that hiring the right people can make a company.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.550
Elon Musk personally interviews candidates himself,
to this day, for certain high-profile positions.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.610
SpaceX is hiring big time.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:16.890
Musk tweeted his company needs engineers,
technicians, builders, and essential support

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:21.470
personnel in and around southern Texas - home
to its production facility and launch site

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:22.490
for Starship.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:26.020
He expects to bring in several thousand more
people over the next year or two.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.660
But, if you want one of the key jobs, you’ll
have to pass muster.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.619
So, what does Musk look for in job candidates?

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:36.680
According to a biography by Ashlee Vance,
he’s been known to ask people riddles during

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:37.870
job interviews.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.110
Like this: Imagine if you’re standing on the
surface of the Earth.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:45.030
You walk one mile south, one mile west, and
one mile north.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.200
You end up exactly where you started.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.440
Where are you?

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.710
One answer is the North Pole.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.960
If you start there and go a mile south, then
a mile west and then a mile north, you’ll

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.629
make a triangular path and end up back at
the North Pole.

00:00:56.629 --> 00:00:59.110
But there’s actually more than one answer.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.129
Musk has been known to follow up with: Where else could you be?

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:04.600
The answer is somewhere close to the South Pole.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.600
Imagine a circle with a one-mile circumference.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.070
If you started a mile north of the circle
and traveled a mile south, then a mile west

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.760
all the way around the circle, then a mile
north, you’d be back at your starting point.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.400
Musk is said to care less about whether the
person gets the right answer and more about

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.710
how they try to solve the problem.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:25.770
The approach is key as he explained in this
interview with a leading German automobile

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:26.770
magazine.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.770
When I interview somebody, I really just ask
them to tell me the story of their career,

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:32.764
what are some of the tougher problems that
they’ve dealt with, how they’ve dealt

00:01:32.764 --> 00:01:38.170
with those, and how they made decisions at
key transition points.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:42.490
Usually, that’s enough for me to get
a very good gut feel about someone.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.260
And in order to spot someone who is less than
honest, he says he pays attention to whether

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.010
they can describe their solution in detail.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.047
Detectives use the same strategy during interrogations.

00:01:52.047 --> 00:01:55.009
Honesty helped Sundar Pichai land a job at
Google.

00:01:55.009 --> 00:01:58.549
When interviewers asked what he thought of
Gmail the very same day it launched, he said

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:02.580
he couldn’t answer the question because
he hadn’t been able to use the product.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.620
That impressed the interviewers enough to
move him to the next rounds.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.340
Just a decade later, he became CEO.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:13.180
Before even getting a shot at acing an interview,
simply getting an interview could be tough.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.269
Musk has questioned whether one of the greatest
inventors of all time, Nikola Tesla, would

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:21.469
have even gotten to the interview stage at
the company that now bears his name.

00:02:21.469 --> 00:02:25.989
The Tesla CEO has even said: This is actually
one of my big worries. If Nikola

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:28.439
Tesla was alive today, could he get an interview?

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:31.109
And if not, we're doing something wrong.

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:33.519
And I'm not totally sure he would get an interview.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:37.829
In order to minimize the chance that brilliant
people aren’t overlooked, he says his companies

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:42.319
don’t always focus on the school candidates
attended or even if they graduated from university.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:46.409
O n Tesla’s career page, the
requirement to be a software engineer for

00:02:46.409 --> 00:02:50.959
its artificial intelligence team is either
a Bachelor of Science in an applicable field

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:54.120
or "proof of exceptional skills"

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.590
something that Musk has repeated over and
over again.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:59.519
We’re looking just for evidence of exceptional
ability.

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:04.180
It’s unclear how many people who lack a
degree have actually been hired at Tesla.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.919
But degree or not, “all must pass (a) hardcore
coding test”.

00:03:07.919 --> 00:03:11.109
Those closest to Musk believe he has a knack
for hiring talent.

00:03:11.109 --> 00:03:15.949
When he started recruiting for SpaceX in the early 2000s, Musk ended up meeting an engineer

00:03:15.949 --> 00:03:19.530
named Tom Mueller through an amateur rocket
club in California.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.169
He spoke with Mueller for hours and knew
he had struck gold.

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:26.680
Mueller has been called the modern-day godfather
of rocket science.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.170
He led the design of SpaceX’s Merlin engines
that power the Falcon line of rockets before

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.340
he retired last year.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.089
Elon was very good at recognizing talent and
hired people that recognized talent.

00:03:37.089 --> 00:03:39.840
I feel like my group, Propulsion, was really strong.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:46.040
Hiring really strong people, train them right,
and it’s hard to beat a small group of really

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.040
smart people.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.688
But that doesn’t mean Musk is easy to have
as a boss.

00:03:49.688 --> 00:03:53.590
Headlines have detailed
complaints by former employees over difficult

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:55.040
working conditions.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.269
Musk is also said to expect a level of excellence
from staff.

00:03:58.269 --> 00:04:02.400
Take it from Dolly Singh - the former head
of talent acquisition at SpaceX.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.459
When someone on the question-and-answer site
Quora asked: What is it like to work with or for

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:07.560
Elon Musk?

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:08.979
She responded:

00:04:08.979 --> 00:04:13.459
Working with him isn’t a comfortable experience,
he is never satisfied with himself so he is

00:04:13.459 --> 00:04:15.930
never really satisfied with anyone around
him.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:21.079
He pushes himself harder and harder and he
pushes others around him the exact same way.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:24.389
The challenge is that he is a machine and
the rest of us aren’t.

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:27.169
So if you work for Elon you have to accept
the discomfort.

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:31.669
But in that discomfort is the kind of growth
you can’t get anywhere else, and worth every

00:04:31.669 --> 00:04:34.169
ounce of blood and sweat.

00:04:34.169 --> 00:04:37.759
Musk has also given his staff advice on how they can be
can be more productive.

00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:41.669
He once reportedly sent an email to Tesla
staff suggesting they try to avoid holding

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:43.319
too many meetings.

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:46.629
Even saying people should feel free to walk
out of a meeting if they’re not adding any

00:04:46.629 --> 00:04:47.729
value.

00:04:47.729 --> 00:04:52.830
Whether you're employed at Tesla or any company,
it’s about working as efficiently as possible.

